  [ok]   Sigma(k) = 2 alpha_par k^2 - (8/3) eps A(X0) k^4  + (k-independent tadpole/mass terms), with alpha_par = Gcal'(X0) + 2 X0 Gcal''(X0)
         the residue is k^0 -- exactly the tadpole sector we declared unreliable.  The k^2 and k^4 coefficients are clean.
  [DERIVED] PRINCIPAL SYMBOL of the lapse equation = -(8/3) eps A(X0) k^4.
  [DERIVED] It is non-degenerate iff eps != 0 -- so at eps != 0 the lapse equation is a FOURTH-order elliptic equation for phi, not second order.  That by itself is fine: phi is still algebraically determined mode by mode.
  [DERIVED] THE REAL CONDITION IS NOT THE PRINCIPAL SYMBOL, IT IS THE FULL SYMBOL.  Eliminating phi divides by Sigma(k).  Sigma(k) = 2k^2[alpha_par - (4/3) eps A k^2] has a REAL ZERO at
  [DERIVED]         k_deg^2 = 3 alpha_par / (4 eps A(X0))
  [DERIVED] which is a real positive wavenumber iff  eps * alpha_par > 0.  At that k the lapse is undetermined, the reduced action is singular, and the Cauchy problem for the constrained system fails.  Since gradient stability requires alpha_par > 0 (part 5/7), SOLVABILITY OF THE LAPSE EQUATION AT ALL k REQUIRES
  [DERIVED]         eps < 0        [Gen-2 condition; note this is OPPOSITE to Gen-1, where tensor high-k stability forced eps >= 0]

